+Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is a **local embedded SQLite index** (chosen over on-site HTTPS directory and managed search service).
+
+### Backend Decision: Local Embedded SQLite Index
+
+| Option | Pros | Cons |
+|---|---|---|
+| **Local embedded SQLite** (chosen) | Predictable sub-250 ms lookups; no service to run; works fully offline; single file to manage; rebuild on import is deterministic | Each kiosk validates and rebuilds its own index on snapshot arrival; index rebuild adds latency to import |
+| On-site HTTPS directory | One centrally maintained index; smaller kiosk updates | Registry lookups depend on lab network; service timeout handling required; network access violates HLD tenet |
+| Managed search service | Less index maintenance; richer searches later | Recurring cost; credentials management; external connectivity; violates HLD tenet |
+
+**Rationale for SQLite**: Aligns with the HLD tenet that "All serial-number lookup SHALL complete using files on the kiosk; no runtime network access is permitted." The index rebuild cost is acceptable: 90,000 records at ~150 MB rebuilds in <2 seconds on kiosk hardware, and import is an attended operation.
+
+### SQLite Backend Design
+
+- **Database file**: Single `.sqlite` file per snapshot, stored alongside the snapshot JSON. Named `registry-<snapshotDate>.sqlite`.
+- **Schema**: One table `equipment(serial TEXT PRIMARY KEY, calibration_date TEXT, due_date TEXT, description TEXT, snapshot_date TEXT)` with an index on `serial` (primary key covers it).
+- **Index build**: During snapshot import, after validation passes, stream records into a new SQLite file in a transaction. On commit, atomically replace the active database file (rename). The previous database file is retained until the next successful import.
+- **Lookup**: Prepared statement `SELECT calibration_date, due_date, description, snapshot_date FROM equipment WHERE serial = ?`. Normalization (whitespace trim, ASCII uppercase) applied in application code before the query.
+- **Concurrency**: Read-only lookups; single-writer import. SQLite's WAL mode enables concurrent readers during import without blocking.
+- **Failure modes**: Corrupt database file on disk → treat as missing, fall back to previous snapshot's database. Import interruption → partial database file cleaned up on next import attempt.
 
