 Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index | On-site HTTPS directory; Managed search service | Aligns with HLD tenet: all lookups from local files, no runtime network access. Predictable sub-250 ms lookups; no service to run; single file to manage. Index rebuild on import is deterministic and acceptable (<2 s for 90k records). |
+| Index activation | Atomic rename after transaction commit | In-place update; symlink swap | Rename is atomic on POSIX; no partial-state exposure; simple rollback by retaining previous file. |
+| Concurrency model | SQLite WAL mode; readers never blocked by import | Exclusive lock during import; separate read replica | WAL allows concurrent reads during write; no additional infrastructure; import is attended so brief writer contention is acceptable. |
+| Corrupt database handling | Fall back to previous snapshot's database | Fail closed; attempt repair | Fail-closed would leave kiosk unusable; repair is unreliable for SQLite corruption. Previous snapshot is known-good. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+
+1. ✅ Lookup backend: local embedded SQLite (aligned with HLD tenet, predictable performance)
+2. ✅ Index activation: atomic rename (simple, atomic, rollback-friendly)
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. Index rebuild progress reporting to UI during import (attended operation; current design rebuilds silently)
+2. Maximum snapshot size before streaming import becomes mandatory (current 150 MB is within single-transaction range)
+3. Database vacuum schedule — not needed initially since each import creates a fresh file
